Original commit body from 426dd525d54d492a36c36999886d23d37e89cb3a:

Erlang behaviours were modelled only through the OTP_BEHAVIOUR_CALLBACKS
table: `-callback` declared nothing, and a custom `-behaviour(X)` produced
one `imports` edge. The Elixir analyzer did not read `@behaviour :atom`
(an Erlang module) at all.

Erlang now gets the shape WI-vitas gave Elixir:
- `-callback name(Args) -> T.` (both spellings, multi-clause specs) is a
  `name/arity` function symbol with modifiers=["abstract"],
  meta["optional"] when `-optional_callbacks` lists it, kept out of every
  call lookup and of global_symbols.
- `-behaviour(X)` emits `implements` module -> every in-repo module X, else
  `erlang:external:0-0:X:unresolved` (OTP behaviours included, so
  owner_ancestry sees the library ancestor; the OTP rooting is kept), and
  each X callback `dispatches_to` the same-name/arity function.

Elixir reads `@behaviour :gen_cycle` (atom, colon stripped, never
alias-expanded) to an unresolved implements target and roots OTP callbacks
for `@behaviour :gen_statem`. `use :atom` stays unread (an Erlang module
cannot define __using__; 0 corpus sites).

New Bridge linker `beam-behaviour-linker` (elixir+erlang) resolves an
Elixir atom stub to the in-repo Erlang module and links each Erlang
callback to the Elixir function admitting its arity.

The shared edge shapes and the callback stable_id live in
hypergumbo_core.behaviours. Callback stable_ids are minted by the
producer: the backstop floor collided with a same-named function in the
behaviour module and nulled both (an analogue that also hit Elixir
@callback; fixed there too).

Erlang joins the interface-dispatch parity column (hard pass).

Measured: cowboy 0 -> 62 implements, 90 callback dispatches; ejabberd
0 -> 242 implements, 1283 erlang->erlang and 14 erlang->elixir callback
dispatches; plausible's 3 atom behaviour sites now emit implements.

